NAME
    compress, decompress - shrink files with the data card

SYNOPSIS
    compress   <file> [out] [-k]
    decompress <file.tcz> [out] [-k]

DESCRIPTION
    compress deflates a file into a self-describing .tcz container using the
    data card's hardware deflate (available on every data-card tier).
    decompress reverses it. By default the source file is replaced by the
    output; pass -k (--keep) to keep the original.

    With no second argument, compress writes <file>.tcz and decompress writes
    the name with the .tcz suffix stripped.

    These commands require a data card. Without one they refuse rather than
    "compress" something that would save nothing — a .tcz that was merely
    STORED (because the data didn't shrink, e.g. already-compressed images)
    still reads back without a card, but freshly compressing needs one.

OPTIONS
    -k, --keep   Keep the source file instead of replacing it.

NOTES
    A data-card call draws from a per-tick budget and can briefly pause the
    computer on very large files, so this is best for cold, occasional data,
    not files you rewrite constantly. Tiny or incompressible files are stored
    verbatim (you'll see "stored uncompressed").

    Disk swap (/var/swap) compresses its spilled data automatically when a
    data card is present — see 'man swap'.

EXAMPLES
    compress /home/me/log.txt              (-> log.txt.tcz, original removed)
    compress /home/me/log.txt -k           (keep the original too)
    decompress /home/me/log.txt.tcz        (-> log.txt)

SEE ALSO
    man swap, man memory, MANUAL.md (Chapter 14: compress / decompress)
